==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 09 NOTES — netcat Part 2
Date: 07-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- nc -lvnp <port> -e /bin/bash pipes a shell through the connection (if -e is supported)
- Many modern netcat builds deliberately REMOVE -e because of its abuse potential (reverse shells)
- File transfer via netcat: receiver redirects (>) to a file, sender redirects (<) a file in
- This method has NO integrity check, no resume, no encryption - unlike scp/rsync (Day 20 of Linux journal)
- The basic listener + connect mechanism (Day 08 Part C) is the foundation both file transfer and shells build on
- Recognizing netcat-style listeners/reverse shells defensively ties back to ss -tuln, ps aux, auth.log

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] nc -lvnp 4444 -e /bin/bash  (or noting it's unsupported)
[ ] nc -lvnp 4444 > file  /  nc host 4444 < file  (file transfer)
[ ] nc -lvnp 5555  +  nc 127.0.0.1 5555  (two-way chat)

paste your real output here :
$ nc 127.0.0.1 4444 -e /bin/bash  (worked, or error message?)
> 

$ cat received_file.txt  (did it match the original?)
> 


